1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,175
Somewhere in the waters off Southeast Asia, ships pick up a strange distress call.

2
00:00:05,675 --> 00:00:09,951
A radio operator says the captain and all the officers are dead.

3
00:00:10,171 --> 00:00:11,926
He says the crew may be dead too.

4
00:00:12,146 --> 00:00:13,790
Then the Morse code breaks off.

5
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:15,788
And the last two words come through.

6
00:00:16,288 --> 00:00:16,971
I die.

7
00:00:17,471 --> 00:00:24,452
Rescuers find a cargo ship with every man aboard dead, their faces frozen in terror, even the dog.

8
00:00:24,672 --> 00:00:26,918
Then the ship catches fire and explodes.

9
00:00:27,138 --> 00:00:28,112
Spooky, right?

10
00:00:28,332 --> 00:00:29,471
Here's the problem.

11
00:00:29,691 --> 00:00:32,110
Nobody can prove the ship ever existed.

12
00:00:33,510 --> 00:00:38,798
Hi, I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and today's file is the Ourang Medan.

13
00:00:39,018 --> 00:00:41,141
The most famous death ship on the internet.

14
00:00:41,641 --> 00:00:44,237
You've probably seen it in a creepy list video.

15
00:00:44,457 --> 00:00:47,851
Ten unexplained ghost ships, number three will shock you.

16
00:00:48,071 --> 00:00:51,364
Well, today we're going to do the thing those lists never do.

17
00:00:51,584 --> 00:00:52,740
Check the paperwork.

18
00:00:53,240 --> 00:01:00,086
We'll tell the legend properly, then trace where it came from, and see whether there's a real ship anywhere underneath it.

19
00:01:01,486 --> 00:01:07,193
Let's start with the most popular version of the story, the one that's been repeated for decades.

20
00:01:07,693 --> 00:01:09,496
It's the late nineteen forties.

21
00:01:09,716 --> 00:01:16,859
A Dutch cargo ship called the Ourang Medan is sailing through the Strait of Malacca, between Malaysia and Sumatra.

22
00:01:17,079 --> 00:01:19,407
One of the busiest shipping lanes in the world.

23
00:01:19,907 --> 00:01:22,848
Ships nearby pick up a desperate distress signal.

24
00:01:23,068 --> 00:01:23,972
Morse code.

25
00:01:24,192 --> 00:01:30,122
The message says the captain and all officers are lying dead in the chartroom and on the bridge.

26
00:01:30,342 --> 00:01:31,916
Probably the whole crew dead.

27
00:01:32,416 --> 00:01:34,660
Then some confused, garbled code.

28
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,158
And finally, I die.

29
00:01:36,378 --> 00:01:37,382
Then silence.

30
00:01:37,882 --> 00:01:40,792
As far as last words go, that's very efficient.

31
00:01:41,012 --> 00:01:42,549
No wasted characters.

32
00:01:43,949 --> 00:01:48,974
In the legend, an American merchant ship called the Silver Star races to the position.

33
00:01:49,194 --> 00:01:51,047
Her crew board the Ourang Medan.

34
00:01:51,547 --> 00:01:53,203
Every man on board is dead.

35
00:01:53,423 --> 00:01:58,811
Lying on their backs, eyes wide open, arms reaching up, faces twisted in fear.

36
00:01:59,031 --> 00:02:03,058
The radio operator is still at his post, hand on the transmitter.

37
00:02:03,558 --> 00:02:06,680
Even the ship's dog is dead, frozen mid snarl.

38
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:08,588
And here's the weird part.

39
00:02:08,808 --> 00:02:09,743
No wounds.

40
00:02:09,963 --> 00:02:11,207
No sign of a fight.

41
00:02:11,427 --> 00:02:13,499
No obvious cause of death at all.

42
00:02:13,999 --> 00:02:17,234
It's the most terrifying crime scene in maritime history.

43
00:02:17,454 --> 00:02:20,664
Which, as we'll see, is suspicious all by itself.

44
00:02:22,064 --> 00:02:24,640
The rescuers decide to tow the ship to port.

45
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:26,059
They attach a line.

46
00:02:26,279 --> 00:02:29,644
And then they notice smoke coming from cargo hold number four.

47
00:02:30,144 --> 00:02:31,560
The fire spreads fast.

48
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:35,884
The boarding party scrambles back to their own ship and cuts the tow line.

49
00:02:36,384 --> 00:02:39,678
Moments later, the Ourang Medan explodes and sinks.

50
00:02:39,898 --> 00:02:44,011
Taking every body, every clue, and every piece of evidence with it.

51
00:02:44,511 --> 00:02:45,373
Convenient.

52
00:02:45,593 --> 00:02:46,828
Really convenient.

53
00:02:47,048 --> 00:02:52,391
If you were writing a mystery that nobody could ever check, this is exactly how you'd end it.

54
00:02:53,791 --> 00:02:54,465
Okay.

55
00:02:54,685 --> 00:02:55,714
That's the legend.

56
00:02:55,934 --> 00:02:59,017
Now let's put on our boring investigator glasses.

57
00:02:59,237 --> 00:03:00,498
Mine are permanent.

58
00:03:00,998 --> 00:03:03,242
A real shipwreck leaves a paper trail.

59
00:03:03,462 --> 00:03:04,634
Insurance claims.

60
00:03:04,854 --> 00:03:05,949
Company records.

61
00:03:06,169 --> 00:03:06,990
Crew lists.

62
00:03:07,210 --> 00:03:08,525
Official registers.

63
00:03:08,745 --> 00:03:10,730
Navy and coast guard reports.

64
00:03:10,950 --> 00:03:12,104
Grieving families.

65
00:03:12,604 --> 00:03:14,870
So let's go looking for that paper trail.

66
00:03:15,090 --> 00:03:15,920
And spoiler.

67
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:18,864
It's the paper trail that's the real mystery here.

68
00:03:20,264 --> 00:03:26,341
Big merchant ships were listed in registers like Lloyd's Register, and in national ship records.

69
00:03:26,841 --> 00:03:30,037
Researchers have searched those records for an Ourang Medan.

70
00:03:30,257 --> 00:03:31,503
They haven't found one.

71
00:03:31,723 --> 00:03:33,187
No construction record.

72
00:03:33,407 --> 00:03:34,669
No registration.

73
00:03:34,890 --> 00:03:35,732
No owner.

74
00:03:36,232 --> 00:03:40,195
And the Silver Star, the American ship that supposedly found her?

75
00:03:40,415 --> 00:03:45,004
Researchers haven't found any record of a rescue like this in her logs either.

76
00:03:45,504 --> 00:03:54,512
So we have a famous rescue that the rescue ship didn't write down, of a ship that no register knows about, which then conveniently blew up.

77
00:03:54,732 --> 00:03:55,972
That's not a ghost ship.

78
00:03:56,192 --> 00:03:57,360
That's a ghost story.

79
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:00,496
So where did the story come from?

80
00:04:00,716 --> 00:04:03,006
This is where it gets genuinely interesting.

81
00:04:03,506 --> 00:04:06,698
Versions of it appear in newspapers in nineteen forty.

82
00:04:06,918 --> 00:04:09,770
One appears in an Italian newspaper in Trieste.

83
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:15,742
British papers, including the Daily Mirror, printed it, attributed to the Associated Press.

84
00:04:16,242 --> 00:04:19,177
In those early versions, the details are different.

85
00:04:19,397 --> 00:04:20,895
The location changes.

86
00:04:21,115 --> 00:04:25,880
Some put it near the Marshall Islands or the Solomon Islands, not Malacca.

87
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:35,084
Then, in nineteen forty eight, the story pops up again in a Dutch language newspaper in the Dutch East Indies, and in an American paper.

88
00:04:35,304 --> 00:04:36,716
Now with more details.

89
00:04:36,936 --> 00:04:37,812
More drama.

90
00:04:38,312 --> 00:04:39,442
Notice the pattern.

91
00:04:39,662 --> 00:04:44,097
Every time the story is retold, it gets scarier and more detailed.

92
00:04:44,317 --> 00:04:47,408
That's the opposite of how real events usually work.

93
00:04:47,628 --> 00:04:49,659
Real events get fuzzier with time.

94
00:04:49,879 --> 00:04:51,247
Legends get sharper.

95
00:04:52,647 --> 00:04:56,070
And then, the moment that made the legend official sounding.

96
00:04:56,290 --> 00:05:05,002
In nineteen fifty two, a United States Coast Guard publication, the Proceedings of the Merchant Marine Council, included the story.

97
00:05:05,502 --> 00:05:07,987
For a lot of later writers, that was proof.

98
00:05:08,207 --> 00:05:10,865
The Coast Guard printed it, so it must be true.

99
00:05:11,365 --> 00:05:16,810
But a publication repeating a story is not the same as an investigation confirming it.

100
00:05:17,030 --> 00:05:20,529
There's no sign the Coast Guard investigated anything.

101
00:05:20,749 --> 00:05:24,590
It looks like they retold a tale that was already going around.

102
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:30,074
It's the nineteen fifties version of, I saw it on a pretty official looking website.

103
00:05:31,474 --> 00:05:35,902
Now, people who believe the ship was real have proposed explanations.

104
00:05:36,122 --> 00:05:37,765
Let's be fair and look at them.

105
00:05:38,265 --> 00:05:40,094
Theory one, the most popular.

106
00:05:40,314 --> 00:05:41,550
Smuggled chemicals.

107
00:05:41,770 --> 00:05:51,708
The idea is that the Ourang Medan was secretly carrying something dangerous, like potassium cyanide and nitroglycerin, perhaps for a black market.

108
00:05:52,208 --> 00:05:58,304
Seawater leaking into the hold reacts with the chemicals, releasing toxic gas that kills the crew.

109
00:05:58,524 --> 00:06:00,791
Then the chemicals catch fire and explode.

110
00:06:01,012 --> 00:06:04,788
Writer Roy Bainton is among those who explored this idea.

111
00:06:05,288 --> 00:06:06,209
Theory two.

112
00:06:06,429 --> 00:06:10,880
Leftover wartime nerve agents, being smuggled after World War Two.

113
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:15,391
There's no real evidence for this one, but it shows up a lot online.

114
00:06:15,891 --> 00:06:16,845
Theory three.

115
00:06:17,065 --> 00:06:21,126
Carbon monoxide from a faulty boiler or a slow, hidden fire.

116
00:06:21,346 --> 00:06:26,840
It's invisible, it kills quietly, and it could explain a crew dying without wounds.

117
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:29,347
Author Vincent Gaddis suggested this.

118
00:06:30,747 --> 00:06:31,823
And theory four.

119
00:06:32,043 --> 00:06:34,355
The one most researchers now land on.

120
00:06:34,575 --> 00:06:36,793
The Ourang Medan never existed.

121
00:06:37,013 --> 00:06:40,501
It's a piece of fiction or a hoax that grew in the retelling.

122
00:06:41,001 --> 00:06:43,059
Here's the thing about the other theories.

123
00:06:43,279 --> 00:06:46,174
They're all trying to explain the details of the story.

124
00:06:46,394 --> 00:06:47,273
The faces.

125
00:06:47,493 --> 00:06:48,244
The dog.

126
00:06:48,464 --> 00:06:49,240
The fire.

127
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:58,607
But if the story itself isn't reliable, then explaining its details is like explaining how the Big Bad Wolf managed to blow down a brick house.

128
00:06:59,107 --> 00:07:01,480
You can come up with a really clever answer.

129
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,515
It's still a story about a wolf.

130
00:07:04,915 --> 00:07:08,259
So why has this story survived for more than eighty years?

131
00:07:08,759 --> 00:07:10,164
Because it has everything.

132
00:07:10,384 --> 00:07:11,506
A last message.

133
00:07:11,726 --> 00:07:13,076
A terrifying image.

134
00:07:13,296 --> 00:07:14,502
A secret cargo.

135
00:07:14,722 --> 00:07:16,992
A cover up ending where the evidence sinks.

136
00:07:17,492 --> 00:07:19,971
And because the sea is genuinely mysterious.

137
00:07:20,191 --> 00:07:22,794
Real ships really have been found abandoned.

138
00:07:23,014 --> 00:07:26,537
Real crews really have died from gas leaks and fires.

139
00:07:26,757 --> 00:07:31,808
So the Ourang Medan feels possible, even if this particular ship isn't real.

140
00:07:32,308 --> 00:07:34,048
It's basically creepypasta.

141
00:07:34,268 --> 00:07:36,654
The original version, before the internet.

142
00:07:36,874 --> 00:07:40,511
Passed from newspaper to magazine to book to list video.

143
00:07:40,731 --> 00:07:44,425
Each one adding a little more fear and a little less evidence.

144
00:07:45,825 --> 00:07:50,240
So here's a little toolkit, so you can check the next ghost ship story yourself.

145
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:51,530
Step one.

146
00:07:51,750 --> 00:07:55,284
Does the ship have a registry entry, a builder, or an owner?

147
00:07:55,504 --> 00:07:58,690
Real ships leave paperwork from the day they're built.

148
00:07:59,190 --> 00:08:00,015
Step two.

149
00:08:00,235 --> 00:08:03,979
Is there a first source, written at the time, by someone who was there?

150
00:08:04,199 --> 00:08:07,196
Or does every version say, according to reports?

151
00:08:07,696 --> 00:08:08,591
Step three.

152
00:08:08,811 --> 00:08:11,339
Do the details stay the same between versions?

153
00:08:11,559 --> 00:08:13,827
Real events get blurrier over time.

154
00:08:14,047 --> 00:08:15,661
Legends get more specific.

155
00:08:16,161 --> 00:08:17,034
Step four.

156
00:08:17,254 --> 00:08:19,529
Does the ending destroy all the evidence?

157
00:08:19,749 --> 00:08:21,291
Sinking, burning, vanishing.

158
00:08:21,511 --> 00:08:25,780
If the story conveniently can't be checked, that's not proof it's false.

159
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,799
But it is a reason to be careful.

160
00:08:28,299 --> 00:08:32,895
Run the Ourang Medan through that list, and it fails almost every step.

161
00:08:33,115 --> 00:08:34,851
Which, honestly, is impressive.

162
00:08:35,071 --> 00:08:37,462
It takes real effort to be that mysterious.

163
00:08:38,862 --> 00:08:40,587
So here's the honest version.

164
00:08:41,087 --> 00:08:42,025
Documented.

165
00:08:42,245 --> 00:08:53,812
A story about a death ship appeared in newspapers in nineteen forty and again in nineteen forty eight, and was retold in a US Coast Guard publication in nineteen fifty two.

166
00:08:54,032 --> 00:08:56,132
The details change between versions.

167
00:08:56,632 --> 00:08:57,564
Not found.

168
00:08:57,784 --> 00:09:06,028
Any record of a ship called the Ourang Medan, any registration, any owner, or any rescue in the Silver Star's records.

169
00:09:06,528 --> 00:09:07,258
Legend.

170
00:09:07,478 --> 00:09:16,018
The terrified faces, the snarling dog, the secret cargo, the final words I die, and the explosion that erased the evidence.

171
00:09:17,418 --> 00:09:18,530
So, your verdict.

172
00:09:18,750 --> 00:09:25,071
Real death ship covered up by smugglers, or the best ghost story the twentieth century ever told?

173
00:09:25,571 --> 00:09:26,816
Tell me in the comments.

174
00:09:27,036 --> 00:09:32,669
And if you see this ship in your next creepy list video, you'll know the one question they never ask.

175
00:09:32,889 --> 00:09:34,119
Where's the paperwork?

176
00:09:34,619 --> 00:09:38,697
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

177
00:09:38,917 --> 00:09:43,299
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
